<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!-- Privacy manifest for the MorkeTunnel iOS APPEX bundle (T-REL-9).
	     Separate from the app's (Config/PrivacyInfo.xcprivacy) because a manifest describes
	     the bundle it ships in, and this bundle links different code.

	     All three categories below come from Frameworks/Libbox.xcframework, not from our
	     Swift/C/ObjC — which contains none of these APIs. The shipping ios-arm64 slice is a
	     STATIC archive ("current ar archive") carrying no manifest of its own, so libbox's
	     symbols become this bundle's to declare. Measured on the LINKED Release arm64
	     appex binary 2026-08-31: _stat _fstat _fstatat _lstat _statfs _statvfs
	     _mach_absolute_time all survive linking (nothing is dead-stripped), and there is no
	     getattrlist-family symbol and no UserDefaults reference — so those categories are
	     deliberately NOT declared here.

	     This file is a bundle RESOURCE, not a linked dependency: MorkeTunnel still links only
	     MorkeShared, and the NE memory budget is untouched. -->

	<key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
	<false/>

	<key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
	<array/>

	<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<!-- _stat _fstat _fstatat _lstat, from libbox.
			     C617.1 is "metadata of files inside the app container, app group container, or
			     the app's CloudKit container". SingBoxTunnel.start puts every path libbox
			     touches inside the App Group container: basePath = container,
			     workingPath = container/Working, tempPath = container/Library/Caches, plus the
			     cache_file (cache.db) the config validator allow-lists — see
			     MorkeServices/VPN/TunnelWorkspace.swift, which wipes exactly those paths.
			     NOT DDA9.1 (no file timestamp is ever displayed), NOT 3B52.1 (no document
			     picker), NOT 0A2A.1 (third-party-SDK-only reason; this is the app's bundle). -->
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryFileTimestamp</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>C617.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<dict>
			<!-- _statfs _statvfs, from libbox. The callers in the linked binary are
			     base::SysInfo::AmountOfFreeDiskSpace / AmountOfTotalDiskSpace (the vendored
			     tracing library, checking free space before writing trace files) and
			     sftp.getStatVFSForPath / StatVFS.FreeSpace (a vendored SFTP library's protocol
			     handler), reached through Go's syscall.Statfs. E174.1 is "check whether there is
			     sufficient disk space to write files" — that is what the reachable code does with
			     the API. Its off-device clause holds absolutely here: nothing derived from disk
			     space is logged, surfaced or sent anywhere.
			     NOT 85F4.1 (disk space is never displayed), NOT 7D9E.1 (no bug-report path),
			     NOT B728.1 (not a health-research app).
			     ⚠️ Maintainer: E174.1 also carries "the app must behave differently based on disk
			     space in a way that is observable to users". Morke does not — these are dormant
			     library paths we neither call nor can strip from a vendored static archive. The
			     first clause matches the code's purpose and no other DiskSpace reason fits at
			     all; leaving the category undeclared would instead trip ITMS-91053, since the
			     symbols are provably in the shipping binary. Flagged rather than settled. -->
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryDiskSpace</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>E174.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<dict>
			<!-- _mach_absolute_time, from libbox: Go's monotonic clock (runtime.nanotime, backing
			     timers, deadlines and time.Since) plus the vendored tracing library's clock.
			     35F9.1 is "measure the amount of time that has elapsed between events that
			     occurred within the app or to perform calculations to enable timers" — exactly
			     what a monotonic clock is used for here. Its "may not be sent off-device" clause
			     is satisfied by the project's privacy posture: no per-user analytics, no
			     behavioural tracking, no remote crash reporter, and the operational-health
			     metrics are in-memory + os_log with zero network egress.
			     NOT 8FFB.1 (no absolute event timestamps are calculated or sent off-device),
			     NOT 3D61.1 (no user-submitted bug report carries boot time). -->
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategorySystemBootTime</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>35F9.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>

	<!-- EMPTY, and that is a measured claim rather than a default. The published App Privacy label
	     (read from the ASC web console 2026-08-31) names Purchase History / User ID / Device ID —
	     all three are collected by the APP, whose manifest declares them. This extension collects
	     none of them: it makes no control-plane call at all. MorkeTunnel/ contains no URLSession
	     and no API base URL, and MorkeShared — the only module it links — contains no URLSession
	     either; the sing-box config reaches it through the shared Keychain, written by the app,
	     "never by a fetch from the extension" (T-CFG-41). Apple aggregates the bundles' manifests
	     into one Privacy Report, so declaring the app's three types here as well would over-state
	     what the tunnel does. -->
	<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes</key>
	<array/>
</dict>
</plist>
